    "narrative": "The offensive engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP relies heavily on three key outfielders today, with **Corbin Carroll** leading the pack at a 79.71 efficiency score. His matchup against Mitch Keller is a primary target; the moderate SP_xERA of 4.26 combined with a dynamic platoon advantage gives **Corbin Carroll** the highest floor for production. Close behind is **James Wood**, whose 78.41 score is anchored by an elite 19% boost from the SP skill projection against Simeon Woods Richardson and a high-leverage leadoff spot. **Mickey Moniak** rounds out our elite tier at 75.74; while the park factor at Scott\u2019s venue is a major driver, it is his placement in the #2 hole and a strong platoon split that solidify his position as a must-start.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved balancing the middle infield. **Trevor Story** remains in the lineup despite a sub-50 score (48.97) because he provides necessary defensive stability, even though he faces a challenging 3.02 xERA matchup against Jax. We opted to bench **Jose Caballero** entirely, despite his strong 56.17 base projection. Ultimately, the optimizer prioritized the consistent R/HR/RBI potential of **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Tyler Soderstrom**\u2014who enjoys a solid 67.16 score against Painter\u2014over the speed-first profile of **Jose Caballero**, who is currently listed as a bench option in today\u2019s rotations.\n\nWe are navigating a quiet day for our bench assets, as **Josh Naylor** and **Austin Riley** are off, and **Giancarlo Stanton** remains sidelined on the IL. No extreme weather threats exist, but managers should monitor the late-confirming lineups for **Travis Bazzana** and **Kyle Karros**; their scores (50.56 and 54.64, respectively) are marginal, and should either be dropped from the lineup card, we will be forced to pivot to our deeper bench reserves like **Brice Turang** or **Carson Williams** to maintain our competitive edge in the SB and AVG categories.",
